# Async/await preference

## General rule

Prefer **async/await** over **.then()** for async work. Use `.then()` only when necessary (e.g. returning a promise without awaiting, or chaining in a way that is clearer than async/await).

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
login(email, password)
  .then((result) => { if (result.success) router.push("/"); })
  .catch(() => setApiError("Failed"))
  .finally(() => setIsLoading(false));

// ✅ GOOD
const result = await login(email, password);
if (result.success) {
  router.push("/");
}
```

See `src/app/(not-authenticated)/login/_components/LoginForm.tsx`.

## React hooks (useEffect, etc.)

Inside a React hook, **do not** make the effect callback async. Define an **async function inside** the effect, use **try/catch/finally**, then **call** that function.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD – effect callback async or .then in effect
useEffect(() => {
  getData(id).then(setData).catch(setError).finally(() => setIsLoading(false));
}, [id]);

// ✅ GOOD – inner async function + call
useEffect(() => {
  const fetchData = async () => {
    try {
      setIsLoading(true);
      const profile = await getData(id);
      if (!cancelled) {
        setData(profile);
      }
    } catch (error) {
      console.error("Error fetching data:", error);
      if (!cancelled) {
        setError("Failed to load");
      }
    } finally {
      if (!cancelled) {
        setIsLoading(false);
      }
    }
  };
  fetchData();
}, [id]);
```

Prefer Convex `useQuery` for live reads instead of fetching inside `useEffect`. Use the inner-async pattern for one-shot mutations, `localStorage`, or non-Convex work (see `AuthContext` session hydrate).

Use **cancellation** (`let cancelled = false; return () => { cancelled = true; };`) when the effect can re-run before the previous async work finishes.
